{
  "url": "/2026/09/09/3.-Cauchy-Sequences.html",
  "title": "3.3 Cauchy Sequences",
  "lang": "en",
  "description": "Convergence describes the asymptotic behavior of a sequence by relating its terms to a point of the ambient space. This raises a natural question. How much of this asymptotic behavior can be recognized from the terms of the sequence themselves, without first specifying or establishing the existence of such a point? The concept of Cauchyness isolates precisely this internal aspect of convergence. Instead of comparing the terms $x_n$ with a separate point $x$, it compares sufficiently late terms with one another and asks whether they eventually become arbitrarily close.",
  "published_at": "2026-09-09T15:00:00+00:00",
